[size=4][b]How it works[/b][/size]

Every player attack starts its animation through one function inside the game. CCS hooks that function and swaps the animation at the moment the attack starts: one callback per swing, nothing per frame in combat. CCS changes no game file on disk.

Enemy animations play through a cleaned copy with the parts meant only for the AI taken out (its warps onto whoever hit it, its event markers), so they do not drag you across the arena or read state a player does not have. The move's own turn window stays.

With Feel set to Move's own, the parts of your weapon's attack that the game needs travel with the swap: the charge window for long presses, the turn window when the move has none, the weapon's state changes (the Axatana joins into the axe and splits back into katanas on cue) and the elemental Tarstone triggers after each hit. Armor and Steer add the game's own hyper armor, turn window and movement cancel over the move, copied from a player attack that has them, so they behave exactly as they do on your weapon.

[size=4][b]Install[/b][/size]
[list=1]
[*]Close the game.[/*]
[*]Install [url=https://www.nexusmods.com/mortalshell2/mods/45?tab=files]UE4SS for MS2 by Framecore[/url].[/*]
[*]Extract the CCS folder from the ZIP into MortalShell2/Binaries/Win64/ue4ss/Mods/[/*]
[*]Enter the game world, open the Player Menu and switch to the [b]CCS[/b] tab. It is the last tab. There is no hotkey.[/*]
[/list]

CCS goes in:
[code]MortalShell2/Binaries/Win64/ue4ss/Mods/CCS/
    enabled.txt
    core.json
    catalog.json
    enemy-catalog.json
    ranged-catalog.json
    running-catalog.json
    release.json
    README.txt
    THIRD_PARTY_NOTICES.txt
    dlls/
        main.dll
    core/
        ccs_core-1.0.0-alpha.4.dll
    assets/
        banner.png
        enemy-icons/
            *.png
        icons/
            move-fallback.png[/code]

After the first run CCS also creates settings.json, presets/, logs/, runtime/ and CCS.log in that folder.

CCS does not need CSS or CSSX and works alongside both. If they are installed, CCS waits for their tabs and appends itself after them.

[size=4][b]Controls[/b][/size]

The footer of the page always shows the keys for the device you are using.

[b]Controller[/b]
[list]
[*]D-pad left/right: pick a slot. Up/down: move through the candidates.[/*]
[*]A: assign the highlighted move. X: put the weapon's own attack back.[/*]
[*]Right stick click: open the slot's settings on the right. Left/right changes a value, up/down moves between rows. The rows scroll; the focused setting's note stays under them.[/*]
[*]B: back to the slots, then close.[/*]
[/list]

[b]Mouse and keyboard[/b]
[list]
[*]A/D or left/right: pick a slot. W/S or up/down: browse the candidates.[/*]
[*]Space: assign. F: put the weapon's own attack back.[/*]
[*]Tab: the slot's settings on the right.[/*]
[*]The search key shown in the footer puts the caret in the search field; typing filters the list.[/*]
[*]Click rows and buttons, or use the scrollbar. Escape clears the search, then closes the menu.[/*]
[/list]

[size=4][b]Charged attacks[/b][/size]

In Mortal Shell II a charged light attack exists only with the Acolyte's Stone equipped, and a charged heavy attack only with the Unwieldy Stone. The game sets that rule, not the mod: without the stone a long press does the normal attack, and the CCS page tells you which stone the LC or HC slot is waiting for. If you want the slots without the stones, set [b]Charged attacks[/b] to [b]Always (cheat)[/b] in the Settings tab. The charge still costs Resolve.

[size=4][b]Next attack[/b][/size]

Your own combos buffer the next press and fire it right after the hit. Swapped moves with Feel set to Move's own do the same. [b]Next attack[/b] in the Settings tab picks where the buffered press fires: after the move's first hit, like your weapon, or after its last hit, so a long enemy combo plays out before the chain continues.

[size=4][b]Known limits in this alpha[/b][/size]
[list]
[*]The sidearm's fire cannot be customised yet.[/*]
[*]Moves play on the human rig only. When you die and fight on as the Harbinger, or wear a creature shell, the swaps pause until you are back on a human body.[/*]
[*]With Feel set to Move's own, the move keeps its own hit windows and rhythm, so a combo can feel different from your weapon's. Game's keeps your weapon's timing with the new animation fitted in.[/*]
[*]The Clockwork Scythe's chain hits belong to the Scythe's own attack animations and do not follow a swapped move.[/*]
[*]Attacks that are built to break hyper armor still break it, whatever the Armor setting.[/*]
[*]No presets ship with the mod yet. Save your own; I would like to see what you come up with.[/*]
[/list]

[size=4][b]Updating and removing[/b][/size]

Close the game and replace dlls/, core/, core.json and the catalog files. Keep settings.json and presets/ to retain your slots and presets. Slots saved by alpha.1 get Armor and Steer switched on. To remove the mod, delete the Mods/CCS folder. CCS creates settings.json, presets/, logs/ccs.jsonl, runtime/status.json and CCS.log inside its own folder and nothing anywhere else.

[size=4][b]Feedback[/b][/size]

Tell me what breaks, what feels wrong and what you want next. When you report a bug, attach Mods/CCS/logs/ccs.jsonl and say which weapon and which slots were set; that file holds what the mod did on every swing. Ideas for the next versions are as welcome as bug reports.

